{:edb/db-before-t 1723 :edb/db-after-t 1724 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535036 50 #inst "2026-10-08T15:22:31.176Z" 13194139535036 true] [17592186119436 1100 "Find a tangent vector to the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=t\\,\\mathbf i+t^2\\,\\mathbf j+t^3\\,\\mathbf k$ at the point $P(1,1,1)$." 13194139535036 false] [17592186119436 1100 "Find a tangent vector to the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=t\\,\\mathbf i+t^2\\,\\mathbf j+t^3\\,\\mathbf k$ at the point $P(1,1,1).$" 13194139535036 true] [17592186119436 1145 "The point $P(1,1,1)$ is at the tip of $\\mathbf r(t)$ for some specific value $t_0$ of the parameter $t$. At this point, the vector $\\mathbf r'(t_0)$ is tangent to the curve.\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to $P$. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(t) &= i + j + k \\\\\nti + t^{2}j + t^{3}k &= i + j + k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf i, \\mathbf j$, and $\\mathbf k$ yields the following system:\n\n$$\n{t = 1; t^{2} = 1; t^{3} = 1\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $t=1$. This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(1,1,1)$ is at the tip of the vector $\\mathbf r(1)$.\n\nNow, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(t^{2})j + \\frac{d}{dt}(t^{3})k \\\\\n&= i + 2tj + 3t^{2}k \\\\\nr^{′}(1) &= i + 2(1)j + 3(1)^{2}k \\\\\n&= i + 2j + 3k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to the given curve at at the point $P(1,1,1)$ is $\\,\\mathbf i+2\\,\\mathbf j+3\\,\\mathbf k$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1792/Source/Images/1d982638f3bd912bc8329318369e03d5.png)" 13194139535036 false] [17592186119436 1145 "The point $P(1,1,1)$ is at the tip of $\\mathbf r(t)$ for some specific value $t_0$ of the parameter $t.$ At this point,  the vector $\\mathbf r'(t_0)$ is tangent to the curve.\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to $P.$ We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\,i+\\,j+\\,k \\\\ t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j+{t}^{3}\\,k & =i+\\,j+\\,k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf i, \\mathbf j,$ and $\\mathbf k$ yields the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}t=1 \\\\ {t}^{2}=1 \\\\ {t}^{3}=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $t=1.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(1,1,1)$ is at the tip of the vector $\\mathbf r(1).$  \n\nNow, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})\\,k \\\\  & =i+2t\\,j+3{t}^{2}\\,k \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =i+2(1)\\,j+3(1)^{2}\\,k \\\\  & =i+2\\,j+3\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to the given curve at at the point $P(1,1,1)$ is $\\,\\mathbf i+2\\,\\mathbf j+3\\,\\mathbf k.$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/assets/bae639af7c4267014af4e558f9a21b042470a3f7d46fa7a31ac0162c7b08ef08.png)" 13194139535036 true] [17592186119437 1100 "Find a parametrization of the tangent line to the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle \\cos{t}, \\: \\sin{t},\\: 4t^2\\right\\rangle$ at the point where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$." 13194139535036 false] [17592186119437 1100 "Find a parametrization of the tangent line to the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle \\cos{t}, \\: \\sin{t},\\: 4t^2\\right\\rangle$ at the point where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$" 13194139535036 true] [17592186119437 1145 "Let  $P$ be the point at the tip of the vector $\\mathbf r\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right ).$  At this point,  the vector $\\mathbf r'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right )$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf R(u)=\\mathbf r\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right )+u \\, \\mathbf r'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right ), \\qquad u \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we find $\\mathbf r\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,4{(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}⟩=⟨0,1,{\\pi }^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow,  we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,8t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,8(\\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,4\\pi ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the given curve at the point $P$ where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(u)=⟨0,1,{\\pi }^{2}⟩+u⟨-1,0,4\\pi ⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/assets/e24b811fb8d9e43bd0682e3686f8b2ef1198e36016978ca305a7df4dffe21889.png)" 13194139535036 true] [17592186119437 1145 "Let $P$ be the point at the tip of the vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nAt this point, the vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nis tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf R(u)=\\mathbf r\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)+u \\, \\mathbf r'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right), \\qquad u \\in (-\\infty, \\infty)\n$$\n\nFirst, we find\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(\\frac{π}{2}) &= ⟨\\cos (\\frac{π}{2}), \\sin (\\frac{π}{2}), 4(\\frac{π}{2})^{2}⟩ = ⟨0, 1, π^{2}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and evaluate it at\n\n$$\nt=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= ⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(\\cos t), \\frac{d}{dt}(\\sin t), \\frac{d}{dt}(4t^{2})⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - \\sin t, \\cos t, 8t⟩ \\\\\nr^{′}(\\frac{π}{2}) &= ⟨ - \\sin (\\frac{π}{2}), \\cos (\\frac{π}{2}), 8(\\frac{π}{2})⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 1, 0, 4π⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the given curve at the point $P$ where\n\n$$\nt=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\nR(u) = ⟨0, 1, π^{2}⟩ + u⟨ - 1, 0, 4π⟩, u ∈ (- ∞, ∞)\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1792/Source/Images/44a11a13457036bfe5aed9e5772179be.png)" 13194139535036 false] [17592186119438 1100 "Given the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=(t^2+t)\\,\\mathbf i+(t^2-t)\\,\\mathbf j$, find the point at which the tangent to the curve is parallel to the vector $3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j$." 13194139535036 false] [17592186119438 1100 "Given the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=(t^2+t)\\,\\mathbf i+(t^2-t)\\,\\mathbf j,$ find the point at which the tangent to the curve is parallel to the vector $3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j.$" 13194139535036 true] [17592186119438 1145 "First, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(t^{2} + t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(t^{2} - t)j \\\\\n&= (2t + 1)i + (2t - 1)j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j$ are parallel for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r'(t)=c(3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j)\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n(2t + 1)i + (2t - 1)j = c(3i + j)\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\mathbf i$ and $\\mathbf j$ gives the following system:\n\n$$\n{2t + 1 = 3c; 2t - 1 = c\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $c=2t-1$. Substituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2t + 1 &= 3c \\\\\n2t + 1 &= 3(2t - 1) \\\\\n2t + 1 &= 6t - 3 \\\\\n6t - 2t &= 1 + 3 \\\\\n4t &= 4 \\\\\nt &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\mathbf r'$ and $3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j$ are parallel only at the point corresponding to $t=1$.\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(1) &= (1^{2} + 1)i + (1^{2} - 1)j = 2i\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j$ are parallel at the point $(2,0)$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1792/Source/Images/f28e49053aee4f84b3dff0ab2a0ec87d.png)" 13194139535036 false] [17592186119438 1145 "First, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-t)\\,j \\\\  & =(2t+1)\\,i+(2t-1)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j$ are parallel for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that $\\mathbf r'(t)=c(3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j).$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2t+1)\\,i+(2t-1)\\,j=c(3\\,i+\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\mathbf i$ and $\\mathbf j$ gives the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2t+1=3c \\\\ 2t-1=c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $c=2t-1.$ Substituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2t+1 & =3c \\\\ 2t+1 & =3(2t-1) \\\\ 2t+1 & =6t-3 \\\\ 6t-2t & =1+3 \\\\ 4t & =4 \\\\ t & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\mathbf r'$ and $3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j$ are parallel only at the point corresponding to $t=1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =({1}^{2}+1)\\,i+({1}^{2}-1)\\,j=2\\,i,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j$ are parallel at the point $(2,0).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/assets/0f723e89b1c91367d6488e034f8b627b3c406aed6d8d1ffc244d6f014021e04e.png)" 13194139535036 true] [17592186119439 1100 "Given the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle t^2+1\\,,\\, 6t+3\\right\\rangle$, find all of the points at which the tangent to the curve is perpendicular to the vector $\\left\\langle 3\\,,\\, 1\\right\\rangle$." 13194139535036 false] [17592186119439 1100 "Given the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle t^2+1\\,,\\, 6t+3\\right\\rangle,$ find all of the points at which the tangent to the curve is perpendicular to the vector $\\left\\langle 3\\,,\\, 1\\right\\rangle.$" 13194139535036 true] [17592186119439 1145 "First, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= ⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(t^{2} + 1), \\frac{d}{dt}(6t + 3)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨2t, 6⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors\n\n$$\n\\left\\langle 3\\,,\\, 1 \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nand $\\mathbf r'(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\left\\langle 3\\,,\\, 1 \\right\\rangle \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t) = 0\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨3, 1⟩ \\cdot r^{′}(t) &= 0 \\\\\n⟨3, 1⟩ \\cdot ⟨2t, 6⟩ &= 0 \\\\\n3 \\cdot 2t + 1 \\cdot 6 &= 0 \\\\\n6t + 6 &= 0 \\\\\nt &= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only solution is $t=-1$.\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(-1) &= ⟨(-1)^{2} + 1, 6(-1) + 3⟩ = ⟨2,-3⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that\n\n$$\n\\left\\langle 3\\,,\\, 1\\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nand $\\mathbf r'(t)$ are perpendicular at the point $(2,-3)$." 13194139535036 false] [17592186119439 1145 "First, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+1)\\,,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6t+3)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t\\,,\\,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors  $\\left\\langle 3\\,,\\, 1 \\right\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf r'(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $\\left\\langle 3\\,,\\, 1 \\right\\rangle \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t) = 0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨3\\,,\\,1⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨3\\,,\\,1⟩\\cdot ⟨2t\\,,\\,6⟩ & =0 \\\\ 3\\cdot 2t+1\\cdot 6 & =0 \\\\ 6t+6 & =0 \\\\ t & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only solution is $t=-1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-1) & =⟨(-1)^{2}+1\\,,\\,6(-1)+3⟩=⟨2\\,,\\,-3⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $\\left\\langle 3\\,,\\, 1\\right\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf r'(t)$ are perpendicular at the point $(2,-3).$" 13194139535036 true] [17592186119864 1020 #edb/ref 17592186327988 13194139535036 true] [17592186119864 1020 #edb/ref 17592186327995 13194139535036 true] [17592186119864 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328093 13194139535036 true] [17592186119864 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328100 13194139535036 true] [17592186119865 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328002 13194139535036 true] [17592186119865 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328058 13194139535036 true] [17592186119865 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328065 13194139535036 true] [17592186119865 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328072 13194139535036 true] [17592186119865 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328079 13194139535036 true] [17592186119866 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328016 13194139535036 true] [17592186119866 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328037 13194139535036 true] [17592186119866 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328044 13194139535036 true] [17592186119866 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328051 13194139535036 true] [17592186119866 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328086 13194139535036 true] [17592186119867 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328009 13194139535036 true] [17592186119867 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328023 13194139535036 true] [17592186119867 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328030 13194139535036 true] [17592186119867 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328107 13194139535036 true] [17592186120145 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535036 true] [17592186120145 1100 "Find a vector that's tangent to the curve $r(t) = ⟨2(t - \\sin t), 2(t - \\cos t)⟩$ at the point where $t = π$." 13194139535036 false] [17592186120145 1100 "Find a vector that's tangent to the curve $r(t)=⟨2(t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,2(t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩$ at the point where $t=\\pi .$" 13194139535036 true] [17592186120145 1145 "At the point where $t=\\pi $,  the vector ${r}^{′}(\\pi )$ is tangent to the curve.\n\nLet's calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[2(t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)],\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[2(t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)]⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,2(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\pi ) & =⟨2(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ),\\,2(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi )⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2(1-(-1)),\\,2(1+0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to the given curve at the point where $t=\\pi $  is  $⟨4,\\,2⟩$" 13194139535036 true] [17592186120186 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535036 true] [17592186120186 1100 "Given the curve $r(t) = ti + (1 + \\frac{1}{2}t^{2})j$, find all of the points at which $r^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are perpendicular." 13194139535036 false] [17592186120186 1100 "Given the curve $r(t)=t\\,i+(1+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2})\\,j$, find all of the points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are perpendicular." 13194139535036 true] [17592186120186 1145 "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =i+t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ [t\\,i+(1+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2})\\,j]\\cdot (i+t\\,j) & =0 \\\\ t\\cdot 1+(1+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2})\\cdot t & =0 \\\\ 2t+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{3} & =0 \\\\ t(4+{t}^{2}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only solution is $t=0$ since $4+{t}^{2}>0$ for any $t.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0)=0\\,i+(1+\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2})\\,j=j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n we conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular at the point $(0,1).$" 13194139535036 true] [17592186327988 1017 #edb/ref 17592186327989 13194139535036 true] [17592186327988 1089 "q-126738" 13194139535036 true] [17592186327988 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535036 true] [17592186327988 1100 "Find a tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=(1-t)\\,i-{t}^{2}\\,j+(2t+1)\\,k$ at the point $P(2,-1,-1).$" 13194139535036 true] [17592186327988 1113 #uuid "2b20167b-4318-505b-9cb5-6c2420c65eed" 13194139535036 true] [17592186327988 1145 "The point $P(2,-1,-1)$ is at the tip of $r(t)$ for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t.$ At this point, the vector ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})$ is tangent to the curve.\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to $P.$ We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =2\\,i-j-k \\\\ (1-t)\\,i-{t}^{2}\\,j+(2t+1)\\,k & =2\\,i-j-k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand equating the coefficients of $i,j,$ and $k$ yields the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}1-t=2 \\\\ -{t}^{2}=-1 \\\\ 2t+1=-1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $t=-1.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(2,-1,-1)$ is at the tip of the vector $r(-1).$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=-1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{t}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t+1)\\,k \\\\  & =-i-2t\\,j+2\\,k \\\\ {r}^{′}(-1) & =-i-2(-1)\\,j+2\\,k \\\\  & =-i+2\\,j+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to the given curve at the point $P(2,-1,-1)$ is $-i+2\\,j+2\\,k.$" 13194139535036 true] [17592186327989 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327990 13194139535036 true] [17592186327989 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327991 13194139535036 true] [17592186327989 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327992 13194139535036 true] [17592186327989 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327993 13194139535036 true] [17592186327989 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327994 13194139535036 true] [17592186327989 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535036 true] [17592186327989 1041 "selection" 13194139535036 true] [17592186327989 1104 #edb/ref 17592186327992 13194139535036 true] [17592186327989 1110 #uuid "a3bc720e-6d